			<div class="content" id="post-content-4864903">
									

<p>Hello Dootboy96! let's take a look :)</p>
<p>SAND</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>The concept:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Elevator Pitch: Three extraterrestrial lifeforms mechanical in nature claiming to be millions of years old and a machine they refer to as &quot;The Forge&quot; have been recently detained. Apparently they are the last remaining members of their species, and the Forge is their last hope of saving it. The Forge is a highly advanced piece of technology that is the robots' main method of reproduction, able to practically 3D print new members of their species under the correct circumstances. According to the three, Earth has the perfect conditions to incubate the Forge. However, the Forge supplies itself with the materials necessary to undergo such processes by absorbing and thaumaturgically converting large amounts of matter. Due to the catastrophic implications of letting a machine like this function on Earth, consuming the world's resources, the Foundation has concluded that the Forge must be destroyed ASAP before a GH-O 'Dead Greenhouse' Scenario could occur. There is only one problem, though: the Forge is nigh-indestructible by conventional means. But the Foundation is quickly looking into ways of solving this problem.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>My main issue here is the Foundations motive to have the machine destroyed, while using it does pose a threat to life on earth, there isn't really any clear indications that the skips were going to use it no-matter what (in fact they sounds very civil). Considering there isn't a clear danger to keeping it around, it sounds more plausible that it would just be kept in containment. -The Foundation would only really destroy something if they absolutely had too, considering the skips sound reasonable/negotiable its odd they would be so set on destroying it.</p>
<p>Along with this, I feel the skips themselves aren't really expanded on or explained much. Currently, they're only really shown fighting off the Foundation? which (to me) makes the narrative feel a bit flat, along with making the 'aliens' themselves feel a bit cliched.- Currently nothing really separates them from the &quot;they're going to invade us&quot; trope.</p>
<p><strong>The narrative:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Central Narrative: The three aliens learn they must race against the clock to breach containment, secure the Forge, and escape the Foundation before the machine is destroyed, sealing their species' fate for good. However, if they successfully incubate the Forge on Earth, it could very well mean the end of organic life as we know it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>As for the narrative, there isn't really enough of an arc here to get an idea of happens or how this would look in a draft. There doesn't seem to be much on where and how the skips were found, what actually takes place (there isn't really a clear progression here?), and what everything is building up to.</p>
<p>In general, I would recommend developing the skips a little more. perhaps show a bit more their journey across space, why and how they found earth and what the consequence of them not operating here would be. Allow the audience to get a feel for what and who they are, rather than just painting them as what essentially boils down to &quot;aliens that are trying to invade earth&quot;.</p>
<p>- perhaps this could work a bit better if the conflict was more focused on the Foundations containment than anything? maybe they only want to leave in search of an easier place to populate, but the Foundation refuses, or is <em>very</em> reluctant to let them leave?</p>
<p><strong>Red or green?</strong><br />
Due to my afore mentioned concerns, no-greenlight.</p>
<p>If you would like me review this (or any concept) further, then please feel free to send me a PM or ask via IRC. Response times generally range from 2-12 hours over PMs. If your looking for additional critiques/perspectives in general, then I would highly recommend contacting the butterfly squad :)</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Cheers,</p>
<p>RED</p>

