<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>I’d say the agent would be questioning the way The Foundation contains humanoid anomalies and the way it treats d-class.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>My tentative recommendation would be to throw together a specific incident of some kind. That way, the holder's &quot;confession&quot; can be more emotional than a monologue about ethics and practices. Bonus points if you can tie it into the theme of subjective good and evil.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Personal destinies are things that people are always meant to do. They may not know on the beforehand, but they always feel very satisfied when they’re done.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If we disregard terminology, is this still destiny at all? Was the 'holder' destined to overthrow the site administrator? Were those small GOIs fated to crumble due to a single silly locket? Or is it just what happens as a consequence of an anomaly? In other words, how large-scale do the effects of personal destinies get before they stop being personal?</p>
</div>
</div>
